WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:20.550
It seems to be that in the Korean Peninsular
the drums of war seem to constantly be rumbling.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:29.410
Alright, so it is true, that this area was
not known for its peacefulness, but the kind

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.650
of tension that the area has been feeling
recently has not existed for some time…

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:37.640
The United States, a country which already
had 30,000 soldiers deployed in the area,

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:42.620
has also deployed several aircraft carriers,
heavy bombers, and even an advanced anti-missile

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:43.730
system in South Korea.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:49.510
It is so advanced that China has even considered
it a threat to their own security.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:55.440
(“China tells US to remove anti-missile
THAAD system from South Korea amid spying

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.440
fears”, &nbsp;The Independent)

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.820
North Korea, on the other hand, keeps carrying
out new tests with ballistic missiles and

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:02.980
progressing with its nuclear programme.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:07.260
It seems that North Korea doesn't really care
about all the international legislation about

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.970
nonproliferation and nuclear testing.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.680
Japan and South Korea are feeling understandably
tense.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.080
They want to know what to do, what decisions
to make, step by step, in order to stop the

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:28.340
growing threat that Kim Jong-Un represents.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:32.859
We already told you in a previous video here
at VisualPolitik about how Japan is seriously

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:37.010
considering putting an end to the pacifist
nature of the Japanese Constitution.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:40.249
And it even seems right not that even in Beijing
they have had enough of Pyongyang…

00:01:40.249 --> 00:01:45.099
(China reportedly sent 150,000 troops to North
Korea's border.

00:01:45.099 --> 00:01:46.099
)

00:01:46.099 --> 00:01:48.869
If this story were a movie, you’d probably
leave the cinema thinking that the writer

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:55.280
had taken too many creative liberties…

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:01.789
&nbsp;
But, what is really happening in this region?

00:02:01.789 --> 00:02:02.969
What can we actually expect?

00:02:02.969 --> 00:02:08.450
Well, that’s what we are going to be talking
about in this Visual Politik video, but before

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:15.450
we get into all of that, let’s get some
perspective.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.540
(WHERE ARE WE?

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.630
AND HOW DID WE GET TO THIS SITUATION?)

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:24.990
North Korea is some sort of nightmare from
the past, it is a country still living in

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:30.030
the Cold War, a country with one of the most
totalitarian governments in the world, and

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:36.160
along with all of that, it’s also one of
the poorest countries on the planet.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.080
(According to the United Nations, 2 out of
5 North Koreans suffer from undernourishment)

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.280
But Kim Jong-Un,the guy who controls the country
with an iron fist, doesn't seem to give a

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.510
damn about any of that.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:52.850
So, it is estimated that Pyongyang started
to develop its nuclear programme around the

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:58.220
1980s, and since then, there have been a few
key situations which have sped up this process:

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:03.820
The first was the fall of the Soviet Union
in 1991, then came George W Bush´s incorporation

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:08.730
of North Korea into the so-called “Axis
of evil” in 2002, and then there was the

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:12.400
coming to power of Kim Jong-Un in 2011.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.670
&nbsp;
We could say that, every time the regime felt

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:19.560
threatened, they decided to accelerate the
process, but we’re going to discuss that

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.960
later in the video...

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:27.010
(The issue is, today, it is estimated that
North Korea holds from 10 to 20 nuclear weapons

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:31.730
and, according to Rand Corporation, if it
doesn´t stop, the North Korean programme

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:35.590
could accumulate around 100 nuclear bombs
by the year 2020.)

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:39.650
But, wait, because this isn't the end of the
story.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:43.840
For now, the technology used in this country
is… well… pretty basic.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.140
It is based on old designs from the Soviet
Union, so the bombs they have developed up

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:54.760
until now don’t work brilliantly.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.260
And THAT is why Pyongyang is now obsessed
with two things:

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.970
On the one hand: attempting to miniaturize
the bombs so they can be placed in missiles.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:08.870
And on the other hand: developing ballistic
missiles capable of attacking targets that

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.570
are thousands of miles away.

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:15.540
And, hey, the last tests they have conducted
indicate they are probably making some important

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.160
advances.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.160
Many analysts, including the Intelligence
Services of South Korea and The United States,

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.960
have in fact considered that North Korea is
already capable of weaponizing short and mid-range

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:31.450
missiles with nuclear warheads, which could
allow them to reach South Korea and Japan.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:34.960
&nbsp;
And that is not all, it seems clear that,

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:39.520
if the programme doesn’t stop, in a few
years, North Korea will be capable of attacking

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.730
the United States of America with ballistic
missiles.

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:46.350
This would be a significant strategic leap
forward for North Korea.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:58.660
And that, dear friends, is a red line which
is causing the Pentagon many, many headaches.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:02.310
So now you might be wondering: Hasn’t the
United States done anything to stop this country?

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:06.980
And, well, the truth is they have tried almost
everything.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:10.960
(THE U.S. STRATEGY)

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:17.740
For years, the United States and South Korea
have tried to persuade Pyongyang to abandon

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:22.120
their plan of developing nuclear weapons,
and they didn't have any problem taking out

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.710
the checkbook in order to do this…

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:30.210
(In the early 90s, in 1994 to be specific,
Bill Clinton signed the agreement framework

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:35.120
by which North Korea commited to cancel his
military plans in exchange for a generous

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.990
fuel help from the United States as well as
assistance to build two nuclear reactors for

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:41.120
civilian use.)

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:46.200
With this agreement, North Korea obtained
the energy they needed to develop their economy.

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:51.210
As for South Korea, besides giving a huge
amount of humanitarian aid, they also committed

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:55.490
to boost and finance North Korea’s economic
development programmes.

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.889
&nbsp;
It all seemed to be going well… for a while…

00:05:57.889 --> 00:06:03.210
However, it all fell to pieces in 2002 when
George W. Bush found out that Pyongyang had

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.310
been fooling them.

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:09.510
In secret, North Korea had kept developing
its nuclear programmes for military purposes.

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:14.340
After more negotiations, they reached a new
agreement and Bush’s administration restarted

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:19.860
their aid program and lifted sanctions…
but a year later, Pyongyang reneged on the

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:20.860
deal.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:24.240
Despite all this, Barack Obama decided to
try yet again:

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:30.240
In February 2012 they reached a new agreement:
North Korea cancelled the nuclear programme

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.310
and the United States restarted (once again)
economic aid.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.919
This time, Pyongyang broke the agreement only
6 weeks later.

00:06:36.919 --> 00:06:40.720
Alright, so we get the picture.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:50.710
Now you might be wondering: Why are they so
insistent on having nuclear weapons at all?

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:55.639
(WHAT ARE THEY LOOKING FOR?)

00:06:55.639 --> 00:07:00.900
The regime of Kim Jong-Un is pretty set on
developing more advanced nuclear weapons,

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.949
and the international community is going to
have to get used to the idea that this is

00:07:04.949 --> 00:07:07.580
not something that they are going to abandon.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:09.720
&nbsp;
But that still leaves us with the question:

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:10.720
Why?

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.490
Well, first of all, because they think nuclear
weapons are essential for their survival.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:18.850
Pyongyang knows that, with nuclear weapons
around, no foreign power, especially the United

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:22.360
States, would dare to intervene in the country.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:27.740
When the United States invaded Iraq, North
Korean leaders were paying attention.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:33.120
For them, the first mistake Saddam Hussein
made was not having nuclear weapons and other

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:34.120
WMDs.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.410
They thought the same thing when Gaddafi’s
regime fell.

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:41.880
Libya had voluntarily cancelled its programme
for the development of WMDs a few years earlier.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:48.500
So, the North Korean leaders think, and, honestly,
they are probably right, that nuclear weapons

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:50.930
are the best guarantee to their staying in
power.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:55.900
Further, the sales of nuclear weapons technology
is one of the main sources of foreign currency

00:07:55.900 --> 00:08:00.520
coming into the country - currency needed
so the elite can maintain their standards

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:01.520
of living.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:05.590
Since the fall of the Soviet Union, North
Korea has become one of the main exporters

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:09.030
of &nbsp;missile equipment, technology and components.

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:12.310
(“US seizes North Korean ship taking missiles
to Yemen”.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:13.310
The Independent)
&nbsp;

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:17.770
Also, indications are that North Korea has
sold nuclear technology to Iran, and advised

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:23.290
Syria in the construction of the nuclear reactor
that Israel bombarded in 2007.

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:27.070
(“Israel strike 'targeted Syrian nuclear
reactor”.

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:28.070
The Telegraph)

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:32.440
It is precisely this risk of proliferation
which is why the United States is trying so

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.110
hard to stop North Korea.

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:37.810
The other reason is, of course, to try to
prevent this country from being capable of

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:41.139
threatening the United States with a nuclear
attack.

00:08:41.139 --> 00:08:43.279
So now we come to China.

00:08:43.279 --> 00:08:48.839
What does China think of all of this?

00:08:48.839 --> 00:08:50.010
(CHINA)

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:54.470
One thing is certain: if it weren't for China´s
support, North Korea would have never developed

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:55.640
its nuclear programme.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:59.730
And it is not just the technological part
that we are talking about, or the support

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:03.150
to avoid international penalties: it’s also
the money.

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:07.620
“China accounts for more than 90% of North
Korea's total trade” DailyNK

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:13.130
In other words, without the support of Beijing,
this country would have collapsed a long time

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:14.130
ago.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:17.770
However, there are some signs which tell us
China is a bit tired of so much provocation,

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:20.970
and they are not happy about those nuclear
missiles either.

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:25.110
It could be said that, for Beijing, Pyongyang
is now a burden now that China has to deal

00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:29.670
with as it attempts to gain influence and
leadership in international politics.

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:32.850
“China takes the driver's seat on free trade”.

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:33.850
DW

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:36.990
Some analysts have even suggested that Beijing
might be losing control over the government

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:41.570
of Pyongyang which is now self absorbed by
their new nuclear capability.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:46.830
Importantly, for the first time, in November
of 2016 China agreed to completely stick with

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:49.940
the international sanctions being levelled
against North Korea.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:54.339
Besides, in the last months, China has forbidden
North Korean coal imports and gas exports,

00:09:54.339 --> 00:09:59.270
and, well, the international community is
holding its breath, that is a significant

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:01.520
punishment for the Kim Jong-Un regime.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:06.290
And there is more: International relations
between these two countries is not at its

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:07.290
best point...

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.910
In fact, since Kim Jong-Un came to power power,
no top-level bilateral meetings have taken

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:16.290
place and there are less and less official
visits between the countries.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:22.220
So, is Beijing really changing its relationship
with Pyongyang?

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:28.630
(THE ROAD TO A SOLUTION?)

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:31.100
It is still soon to know, but don't be fooled.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:35.940
For the time being, China is not going to
let North Korea fall, which is still an ally

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:37.290
for them in the region.

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:40.480
At least, not until they have serious reasons
to do so...

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:45.100
And that is a key point to understand if we
want to find a solution to this crisis:

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:49.660
China needs to be persuaded to try and make
Pyongyang put an end to this, to cancel their

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:52.070
nuclear programme and carry out real reforms.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:54.910
But that won't happen under the current set
up.

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:59.320
That is why many experts think it is time
for the United States to withdraw from Korea

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:03.580
and consider the dismantling the anti-missile
system, and perhaps consider some economic

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:05.010
compensation as well.

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:07.410
&nbsp;
It’s also important that China understands

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:12.050
that the current situation might lead South
Korea and Japan to pursue an arms race, as

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:15.050
well as the option of developing their own
nuclear weapons.

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:19.110
So, now we’ve told you our opinion here
at Visual Politik, and we’d love to know

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:22.899
what you think, click here if you want to
leave your opinion in this quick survey.

00:11:22.899 --> 00:11:25.500
What do you think Trump´s Administration
should do?

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:28.590
Do you think Should North Korea put an end
to its nuclear plans?

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:30.600
Should they even be threatened with war?

